## Organizing Items into Buckets

**Group By** converts a flat Vec into a Map where each key points to a Vec of items that share that key. Think of it as sorting mail into mailboxes by zip code.

### The Group By Pattern

```xiom
type Person = {
  name: Str;
  age: Int;
}

fn group_by_age(people: &Vec[Person]) -> Map[Int, Vec[Str]] {
  let groups: Map[Int, Vec[Str]] = Map[Int, Vec[Str]].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < people.len() {
    let p = match people.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => continue };

    // Get or create the group Vec for this age
    let group = match groups.get(&p.age) {
      Some(g) => g,
      None => {
        let new_group: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
        groups.insert(p.age, new_group);
        new_group
      }
    };

    group.push(p.name);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return groups;
}
```

Wait — there's a problem. Once we `get` the group Vec, modifying it and inserting it back into the Map is tricky with move semantics. Let's use a cleaner approach:

### Cleaner: Rebuild the Group

```xiom
fn group_by_age(people: &Vec[Person]) -> Map[Int, Vec[Str]] {
  let groups: Map[Int, Vec[Str]] = Map[Int, Vec[Str]].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < people.len() {
    let p = match people.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => continue };

    let existing = match groups.get(&p.age) {
      Some(g) => g,
      None => {
        let empty: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
        empty
      }
    };

    // Build updated list
    let updated: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
    let j = 0;
    while j < existing.len() {
      let name = match existing.get(j) { Some(n) => n, None => "" };
      updated.push(name);
      j = j + 1;
    }
    updated.push(p.name);
    groups.insert(p.age, updated);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return groups;
}
```

This rebuilds the Vec each time — not optimal, but correct and clear.

### Using the Grouped Result

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let people: Vec[Person] = Vec[Person].new();
  people.push(Person{ name: "Alice", age: 25 });
  people.push(Person{ name: "Bob", age: 30 });
  people.push(Person{ name: "Carol", age: 25 });
  people.push(Person{ name: "Dave", age: 30 });
  people.push(Person{ name: "Eve", age: 25 });

  let groups = group_by_age(&people);

  // Look up the 25-year-old group
  let age_25 = match groups.get(&25) { Some(g) => g, None => continue };
  io.println("Age 25 count: " + string.int_to_str(age_25.len()));
  return 0;
}
```

### Group By First Letter

```xiom
fn group_by_first_letter(names: &Vec[Str]) -> Map[Str, Vec[Str]] {
  let groups: Map[Str, Vec[Str]] = Map[Str, Vec[Str]].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < names.len() {
    let name = match names.get(i) { Some(n) => n, None => "" };
    let first_char = string.str_substring(name, 0, 1);

    let existing = match groups.get(&first_char) { Some(g) => g, None => {
      let empty: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
      empty
    }};
    let updated: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
    let j = 0;
    while j < existing.len() {
      let n = match existing.get(j) { Some(x) => x, None => "" };
      updated.push(n);
      j = j + 1;
    }
    updated.push(name);
    groups.insert(first_char, updated);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return groups;
}
```

Same pattern, different key — that's the beauty of Group By.

